  "music": "w3-big-tent-bother",
  "authorNotes": "Two players, three checkpoints, four segments — every prop World 3 has, in one long run. Segment 1: a plain trapeze (trapeze-gaunt-1), gets both players moving; relic-w3-08-a waits on pier B, picked up in passing. Segment 2 is the trampoline-into-trapeze escalation from 06 again: trampoline-gaunt-1's springboard bounce is caught by trapeze-gaunt-2 mid-air, landing on pier C. Segment 3 is the gauntlet's centrepiece and its longest gap (1000px, pier C to pier D): cannon-gaunt is the fast route (the same handle-holder-plus-ammunition play as 07 — one player grips the barrel from pier C's edge, the other rides the barrel across, exactly as flagged there, solo self-fire remains technically available per player but the party solve is faster), and trapeze-gaunt-3 into trampoline-gaunt-mid is the slower, cannon-free alternative for a party that has just spent their cooldown or would rather swing it. relic-w3-08-b waits on pier D. pyramid-spot is a floor outline on pier D's plaza, but it is deliberately NOT wired to a `pose` objective — a pose objective only ever checks a single head's distance to one point (engine contract, objectives.ts), which cannot express 'three players stacked at once'. The human-pyramid objective is instead authored as a `snapshot` with `chainedSeats: 3` anchored on pyramid-spot via `nearPropId`, which the engine can actually evaluate: any three (or four) players hand-chained near the outline score it, unlike a plain single-occupant pose. It only scores for parties of three or more, which is the point — it is the game's best photograph and it needs the extra players to take it. Segment 4 closes on familiar ground: trapeze-final4 out to the goal pier. All four gaps carry an assist beam (segment 3's drawn thicker, the usual tell for the hard point) for a party trading the Clock objective for a guaranteed finish this deep into the level."
